                <h2>This Week's Shift: Rising Ad Costs, Diverging Channel Performance</h2>
<p>As we move into the second month of Q2, a clear signal has emerged for D2C e-commerce sellers: traffic costs are entering a new volatility cycle.</p>
<p><strong>On Meta</strong>, CPMs (cost per mille) have risen 8%–15% compared to the same period last month. For popular categories like home goods and 3C accessories, competition has intensified. Two factors are driving this: Q2 is typically peak season for outdoor category campaigns, increasing ad inventory competition; and some sellers shifted from conservative budgets in Q1 to more aggressive spending, elevating overall bid levels.</p>
<p><strong>On TikTok</strong>, traffic dividends persist, but conversion quality is showing significant divergence. Early entrants report high add-to-cart rates from TikTok traffic, but lower first-purchase conversion rates — users tend to "scroll and leave," with longer decision-making cycles. This means TikTok traffic requires a robust retargeting funnel to convert effectively. Relying solely on organic reach or in-feed ads is insufficient to drive GMV directly.</p>
<p>This week, multiple D2C teams raised a central concern: how to allocate budgets across channels. Key questions include: What ratio should guide multi-channel distribution? How much budget is needed to successfully cold-start a channel? And when budgets are spent but ROI falls short, should you increase or decrease spend?</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Dimensions to Clarify Budget Allocation Logic</h2>
<h3>1. Set Channel Ratios by Business Stage</h3>
<p>Budget allocation should not be fixed; it must evolve with your business stage:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Stage</th>
<th>Primary Channels</th>
<th>Recommended Ratio</th>
<th>Goal</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Cold Start (0–3 months)</td>
<td>Meta precise targeting + Google Search</td>
<td>60% / 30%</td>
<td>Accumulate conversion data, refine audience models</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Growth (3–6 months)</td>
<td>Meta + TikTok + Retargeting</td>
<td>40% / 35% / 25%</td>
<td>Test TikTok creative efficiency, build retargeting funnel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Maturity (6+ months)</td>
<td>Channel rotation + platform campaign sync</td>
<td>Dynamic</td>
<td>Maximize channel efficiency, avoid single-channel dependency</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>This week's tactical tip</strong>: If your TikTok has produced 3–5 creatives with ROI &gt; 2, consider raising TikTok's budget share to 40%. If TikTok conversion rates consistently stay below 1%, pull the budget back to 25% or less and prioritize stabilizing your Meta foundation.</p>
<h3>2. Cold Start Budget Threshold Calculation</h3>
<p>The biggest risk in cold start is not "spending too much" — it's "not spending enough before killing the campaign." Ad systems need sufficient impressions to complete the learning phase. Use this formula to estimate:</p>
<pre><code>Cold start budget per channel ≈ Daily target conversions × Cost per acquisition × 7 days × 2 (learning multiplier)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Example</strong>: A utility app targets 10 purchases per day with an expected CPA of $15. Cold start budget per channel should be no less than <strong>$15 × 10 × 7 × 2 = $2,100/week</strong>. Below this threshold, ads may fail to complete learning due to insufficient data, resulting in inflated CPAs.</p>
<h3>3. Signals for Channel Rotation</h3>
<p>When should you pull budget from a channel, and when should you scale up? Monitor two core metrics:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CPA Trend</strong>: If CPA rises consecutively for 5–7 days by more than 20%, and it's not due to creative or audience factors, the competitive environment has shifted. Consider reducing spend or adjusting bidding strategies.</li>
<li><strong>Incremental Cost vs. Incremental Return</strong>: When adding 20% budget to a channel yields less than 15% GMV increase, diminishing marginal returns have set in. Shift budget to channels with higher incremental returns.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>This Week's Action Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Audit CPM changes this week</strong>: If Meta CPMs have risen more than 10%, evaluate shifting to lower-competition time slots (early morning or weekday troughs).</li>
<li><strong>Tier your TikTok creatives</strong>: Rank by CTR, prioritize scaling creatives with CTR &gt; 3%, pause those with CTR &lt; 1%.</li>
<li><strong>Establish a budget review routine</strong>: Dedicate 15 minutes every Friday for a quick channel ROI review, marking "scale," "maintain," or "reduce" actions for each channel, then execute on Monday.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Weekly Takeaway</h2>
<p>Q2 budget allocation is not a static plan — it's a dynamic game. This week's shifts remind us that traffic costs fluctuate with seasons, competition, and platform algorithm changes. Maintaining budget flexibility matters more than clinging to fixed ratios. The core principle is simple: let money flow to where ROI is highest, not where it's most familiar.</p>
